  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=-6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448\u2026\u2248-0.167\\,4\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4+2\\pi =6.115\\,785\u2026\u22486.115\\,8\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & \u22483.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6.115\\,8\\cdot 3.309\\,0=20.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
